  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Marshall Islands Chamber of Commerce (MICC) believes that private sector development is the most important means of addressing the Marshall Islands heavy reliance on imports and aid. However, constraints such as the ease and cost of doing business, a critical shortage of educated and trained personnel, insufficient capital, and limited access to land and resources have impeded private sector growth. 

In Aug 2022, MICC analysed the changing business environment through desk review, key stakeholder interviews, and consultations with 35 member companies. Two issues were explored &#x2013; members views on and experience with national economic policy and on labour market governance. As an outcome of this research, MICC produced a background paper to provide members with an overview of key policies, programs and strategies implemented by the Government in alignment with the national vision of &#x201c;sustainable, equitable and measurable development reflecting the priorities and culture of the Marshallese People&#x201d;. Moreover, this background paper facilitated member preparations for forthcoming consultations aimed at informing the Chamber&#x2019;s position paper. 
In Nov 2022, MICC then published a policy position paper with recommendations addressed to the Government. These recommendations covered diverse areas including tax reform, investment, land access, labour market governance, skills development and labour mobility, and sectoral development/economic diversification. 

Throughout 2023, MICC actively advocated for their recommendations by engaging in various forums and bilateral meetings with Government and other stakeholders. These efforts yielded positive outcomes, fostering a developing policy dialogue on topics such as trade policy and tax reform. Additionally, progress was made in reviewing labour law to ensure alignment with fundamental ILO conventions, and there was agreement to duly consider the private sector&#x2019;s needs in development of a national employment plan. The ILO contributed to strengthening the Chambers capacity for advocacy through technical assistance in the design of the research methodology, technical feedback on drafts produced, and coaching on advocacy strategy. Specifically, the ILO provided technical oversight to the development of the background paper for members on economic and social policy in the Republic of the Marshall Islands, and the Private sector position paper on economic and social policy in the Republic of the Marshall Islands. The ILO also provided the Chamber to review national laws and policy. Specifically, the ILO enhanced the advocacy capacities of MICC President and Vice Presidents to be evidence-based by presenting the importance of data and reflecting members&#x2019; views into its position paper.</narrative>
   </description>
